t: inferred label cannot separate real defensive adjustment from offense executing differently; near-uniform transition matrices consistent with label noise.\n- observed_sample_and_power: 523 cells; 440 pairs\n- auto_validation_status: no_validation_recorded; evidence: NOT_RECORDED"}, {"id": "FAM:SF-APPS-085", "score": 0.104, "document": "# FAM:SF-APPS-085 | Study family SF-APPS-085: Connector teammate-path quality Q_p split-half reliability\n- observed_research_question: Connector teammate-path quality Q_p split-half reliability\n- auto_outcome_status: untestable\n- observed_hypothesis: Connector quality Q_p (teammate-initiated on-off PPP gap) is a stable player-season attribute.\n- observed_result: Pearson r(Q_a,Q_b)=+0.215 p=0.0297; Spearman rho=+0.242. Spearman-Brown Q~0.355, S~0.278. Sign agreement 65.7% (67/102) binomial p=0.000996. ICC(player-season)=0.216 (78.4% within). Attenuation: even a perfect predictor  [cut; read_family for the full text]\n- auto_failure_mechanism: A_unanswerable (question not answerable with the data or design)\n- auto_limited_by: LIM:low_measurement_reliability\n- auto_has_risk: RISK:MEASURE_RELIABILITY\n- observed_failure_evidence: Split-half r=0.215 / SB 0.355 is too low to treat Q_p as a stable scoutable trait; most variance is within-player half-to-half.\n- observed_sample_and_power: n=102 connectors\n- auto_related_family: FAM:SF-APPS-086"}, {"id": "FAM:SF-APPA-034", "score": 0.078, "document": "# FAM:SF-APPA-034 | Study family SF-APPA-034: Opponent-relative leverage prevalence sweep\n- observed_research_question: Opponent-relative leverage prevalence sweep\n- auto_outcome_status: not a hypothesis test\n- observed_hypothesis: How often does an opponent-specific exploitable defensive weakness exist?\n- observed_result: Exploit available 33.3%; trap 23.3%; both 6.7%; neither 50.0%; median best edge 3.79%.\n- auto_failure_mechanism: E_unclear (mechanism unclear from the records)\n- observed_failure_evidence: NOT_RECORDED\n- observed_sample_and_power: 870 pairs\n- auto_validation_status: no_validation_recorded; evidence: NOT_RECORDED\n- observed_method: 870 ordered team pairs; defense PT edge vs league >= +5% on seed PTs.\n- observed_verdict: descriptive\n- observed_failure_type: E_unclear\n- observed_notes: Edges depend only on the defense (e.g. DAL PNR +9.2% for every offense), so they are not matchup-specific.\n- auto_confidence: medium (0.65)\n- auto_addresses_question: RQC:RQC-068\n- auto_uses_dataset: DS:NCAA_tournament_and_ratings_data"}], "n_results": 4}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "defensive performance measurement",
    "within-game performance dynamics",
    "team trait reliability",
    "halftime adjustments"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "how to isolate defensive impact from other factors",
    "how to quantify H2-H1 performance changes",
    "how to assess reliability of a team metric across seasons/games",
    "what are common confounders for within-game performance shifts?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPM-021",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Described how play-type mix shifts by halftime margin, crunch time and after runs. Found OTHER +2.26 to +2.48pp in H2 in every bucket; crunch OTHER +6.08pp, PULL_UP +1.20pp at -0.045 PPP; post-run DRIVE +3.75pp, OTHER -6.72pp."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Hypothesized playoff defenses change scheme within games and between games. Found within-game change 319/523 (61.0%); cross-game change 298/440 (67.7%)."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-085",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Tested if connector quality Q_p (teammate-initiated on-off PPP gap) is a stable player-season attribute. Found Pearson r(Q_a,Q_b)=+0.215 p=0.0297; Spearman rho=+0.242. Spearman-Brown Q~0.355, S~0.278. Sign agreement 65.7%."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-034",
      "status_as_recorded": "not a hypothesis test",
      "finding": "Described how often an opponent-specific exploitable defensive weakness exists. Found exploit available 33.3%; trap 23.3%; both 6.7%; neither 50.0%; median best edge 3.79%. Notes that edges depend only on the defense (e.g. DAL PNR +9.2% for every offense), so they are not matchup-specific."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPM-021",
      "what_failed": "Descriptive analysis of within-game play-type shifts.",
      "why": "No inferential tests; 'OTHER' label known to be noisy (PT audit); low measurement reliability."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "what_failed": "Attempt to test defensive scheme change within and across games using inferred labels.",
      "why": "Inferred label cannot separate real defensive adjustment from offense executing differently; near-uniform transition matrices consistent with label noise. Question deemed unanswerable with the data or design."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-085",
      "what_failed": "Testing split-half reliability of 'connector quality' as a stable player-season attribute.",
      "why": "Split-half r=0.215 / SB 0.355 is too low to treat Q_p as a stable scoutable trait; most variance is within-player half-to-half. Question deemed unanswerable with the data or design due to low measurement reliability."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Measuring within-game tactical adaptations (like H2-H1 changes) is challenging due to noisy labels and low measurement reliability (FAM:SF-APPM-021).",
    "Inferring defensive scheme changes can be confounded by offensive execution and label noise, making it difficult to isolate true defensive adjustments (FAM:SF-APPA-005).",
    "Assessing the reliability of derived metrics (e.g., player-season attributes) using split-half methods can reveal that much of the variance is within-unit, making them unsuitable as stable traits (FAM:SF-APPS-085)."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether 'a defense's effect on opponents' H2-H1 change' can be reliably measured at all.",
    "Specific methodologies for robustly isolating defensive effects from offensive changes in H2-H1 performance.",
    "Whether this specific metric (defense's effect on H2-H1 change) is a stable team trait or if its variance is mostly within-team-season."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The primary challenge lies in rigorously defining and isolating 'a defense's effect' on H2-H1 change, distinct from general game flow, offensive adjustments, or random variance.",
    "Even if a metric for 'H2-H1 change' can be defined, establishing its 'reliability' as a 'team trait' will be difficult given the low reliability observed in similar derived metrics (FAM:SF-APPS-085)."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The precise definition of 'H2-H1 change' (e.g., PPP, offensive efficiency, specific play-type usage) is not specified in the research question.",
    "How 'a defense's effect' will be isolated from offensive adjustments, game state, personnel changes, and other confounding factors is unclear.",
    "The expected magnitude of any H2-H1 change attributed to defense, and thus the required sample size for adequate power, is unknown."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "yes",
    "explanation": "The historical evidence strongly indicates that measuring and attributing within-game changes to a specific defensive effect is fraught with methodological challenges, particularly concerning label noise (FAM:SF-APPM-021, FAM:SF-APPA-005) and the low reliability of derived metrics (FAM:SF-APPS-085). This evidence significantly lowered the probability of testability and shifted the decision from 'proceed' to 'do_not_proceed' without substantial re-scoping."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.05,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "The metric 'defense's effect on opponents' H2-H1 change' is likely to have very low reliability, making it unsuitable as a stable team trait. Historical evidence (FAM:SF-APPS-085) shows similar derived metrics have poor stability, with most variance being within-unit. Isolating the defensive component from other factors further compounds this reliability issue."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "H2-H1 performance changes are subject to numerous confounding factors including offensive adjustments, score effects, substitution patterns, player fatigue, luck, and game flow. It is extremely difficult to isolate the specific causal effect of the defense from these other influences, as noted in FAM:SF-APPA-005 regarding separating defensive adjustments from offensive execution."
      },
      {
        "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
        "text": "If the 'H2-H1 change' metric relies on inferred defensive schemes or noisy play-type labels (e.g., 'OTHER' in FAM:SF-APPM-021), the presence of label error will directly undermine the validity and reliability of the measurement, as highlighted in FAM:SF-APPA-005."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior work directly answered whether 'a defense's effect on opponents' H2-H1 change is a reliable team trait'. However, related studies highlight significant challenges in measuring within-game tactical adaptations, inferring defensive adjustments, and establishing the reliability of derived metrics as stable traits."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The core problem is the reliability of the proposed metric itself. Even if a definition is found, its game-to-game or half-to-half variance may be so high that it cannot be considered a 'reliable team trait'. Historical evidence (FAM:SF-APPS-085) shows this is a common issue for derived basketball metrics."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "It will be challenging to disentangle the 'defense's effect' from a multitude of other factors influencing H2-H1 performance changes, such as offensive adjustments, score effects, substitution patterns, player fatigue, and random variance. This makes causal attribution highly problematic."
    },
    {
      "code": "LABEL_ERROR",
      "text": "If the H2-H1 change or defensive effect relies on inferred labels (e.g., defensive scheme, play types like 'OTHER'), there's a high risk of label error contaminating the measurement, as seen in FAM:SF-APPM-021 and FAM:SF-APPA-005. This noise can mask any true underlying signal."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Given the likely low reliability and high confounding, a very large sample would be needed to detect a statistically significant and meaningful effect, if one exists. The available 120 team-seasons might be insufficient if the effect size is small and noise is high."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.05,
    "rejected": 0.15,
    "untestable": 0.6,
    "artifact": 0.15,
    "uncertain": 0.05
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The split-half reliability (e.g., Spearman-Brown corrected Pearson correlation) of 'defense's effect on opponents' H2-H1 change' across games for the same team within a season, or across seasons.",
    "unit": "Team-game or team-season.",
    "sample": "All available team-seasons (120 units) from 2022-23 to 2025-26. For within-season reliability, split each team's games into two halves (e.g., odd/even games). For cross-season reliability, use consecutive seasons for teams.",
    "power": "Not estimable without a clear definition of the metric and an understanding of its expected variance and effect size. Historical evidence suggests low reliability, implying low power for any reasonable sample size.",
    "controls": "To isolate 'defense's effect' on H2-H1 change, a robust methodology would need to control for: offensive adjustments, score differential at halftime, game pace, opponent strength, home/away status, player availability (injuries/substitutions), and potential regression to the mean. This would likely require a complex causal inference model, possibly using difference-in-differences or instrumental variables, which is not implied by the current question phrasing.",
    "falsification": "A low split-half reliability coefficient (e.g., Spearman-Brown < 0.5) would falsify the hypothesis that it is a 'reliable team trait'.",
    "multiplicity": "N/A, as the primary test is a single reliability coefficient.",
    "decision_rule": "The metric is considered a 'reliable team trait' if its split-half reliability (Spearman-Brown corrected Pearson r) is >= 0.7, with a p-value < 0.05.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25 (complete); 2025-26 regular season and playoffs (partial as of 2026-10-06). Play-by-play, substitution, starters, defensive matchup, box-score data."
  }
}

